In SAS, you can output a PDF file with a journal format using the ODS (Output Delivery System) procedure in conjunction with the PDF destination. Here's a general syntax:

```sas
ods pdf file="your_pdf_file.pdf" notoc;
ods journal off;
ods listing;
  <your SAS code>;
ods listing close;
ods pdf close;
```

Here's what each part does:

* `ods pdf file="your_pdf_file.pdf"`: Specifies the output file name and location. You can modify this to your desired file name and path.
* `ods journal off`: Turns off the journaling of the SAS code and output. This means that the code and output will not be included in the PDF file.
* `ods listing`: Switches the output to listing mode, which is necessary for outputting the journal format.
* `<your SAS code>`: Replace this with your SAS code that generates the output you want to include in the PDF file.
* `ods listing close`: Closes the listing mode and switches back to the default output mode.
* `ods pdf close`: Closes the PDF file and ends the PDF destination.

Here is a complete example:

```sas
ods pdf file="example.pdf" notoc;
ods journal off;
ods listing;
proc print data=sashelp.cars;
run;
ods listing close;
ods pdf close;
```

This code will generate a PDF file named "example.pdf" in the current working directory with the output from the `proc print` statement in a journal format.

You can customize the appearance of the journal format by using various options and statements available with the ODS PDF procedure. For example, you can change the font, font size, margins, and more. Here's an example:

```sas
ods pdf file="example.pdf" notoc;
ods journal off;
ods listing;
ods pdf footnote="Generated by SAS" ;
ods pdf paper="a4landscape" ;
ods pdf bodyfont=accept ; 
proc print data=sashelp.cars;
run;
ods listing close;
ods pdf close;
```

